  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The European Peacock Butterfly (Aglais io) is a striking member of the Brush-footed Butterfly family known for its distinctive eyespots that resemble peacock feathers. This medium-sized butterfly is occasionally found in New England, particularly in gardens and open spaces, though it is more common in Europe.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Eyespots",
      "description": "Four prominent, dark eyespots with blue and yellow centers arranged on reddish-brown wings, resembling a peacock's tail feathers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Wing Color",
      "description": "Deep rust-red or brick-red coloration on the forewings and hindwings with darker borders",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Medium-sized butterfly with a wingspan of 1.75 to 2.5 inches (45-64 mm)",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This butterfly prefers open woodlands, gardens, meadows, and areas with flowering plants and nettles, which serve as host plants for their caterpillars.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "When threatened, the Peacock Butterfly can flash its eyespots suddenly, which startles predators by mimicking the eyes of a much larger animal, giving it a chance to escape.",
